Sentence 1: Monocotyledon
a stem (secondary growth) via the same kind of vascular cambium found in non-monocot woody plants.
Sentence 2: However, some monocots do have secondary growth, and because it does not arise from a single vascular cambium producing xylem inwards and phloem outwards, it is termed "anomalous secondary growth".
Sentence 3: Examples of large monocots which either exhibit secondary growth, or can reach large sizes without it, are palms (Arecaceae), screwpines (Pandanaceae), bananas (Musaceae), "Yucca", "Aloe", "Dracaena", and "Cordyline".
Sentence 4: The monocots form one of five major lineages of mesangiosperms (core angiosperms), which in themselves form 99.95% of all angiosperms.
