Hindi Hindi (Devanagari: हिन्दी, "Hindī"), or Modern Standard Hindi (Devanagari: मानक हिन्दी, "Mānak Hindī") is a standardised and Sanskritised register of the Hindustani language.
Sentence 2: Hindi written in the Devanagari script is one of the official languages of India, along with the English language.
Sentence 3: It is one of the 22 scheduled languages of the Republic of India.
Sentence 4: However, it is not the national language of India because no language was given such a status in the Indian constitution.
Sentence 5: It is a pidgin that stemmed from Hindi and included vocabulary from several other languages, such as Bengali, Dimasa and the Zeme Naga dialect.
Sentence 6: Linguistically, Hindi and Urdu are two registers of the same language and are mutually intelligible.
Sentence 7: Hindi is the most commonly used official language in India.
Sentence 8: Urdu is the and "lingua franca" of Pakistan and is one of 22 official languages of India.
Sentence 9: The splitting of Hindi and Urdu into separate languages is largely motivated by politics,
