Sentence 2: After 1924, Ellis Island became primarily a detention and deportation processing station.
Sentence 3: During and immediately following World War II, Ellis Island was used to hold German merchant mariners and "enemy aliens"—Axis nationals detained for fear of spying, sabotage, and other fifth column activity.
Sentence 4: In December 1941, Ellis Island held 279 Japanese, 248 Germans, and 81 Italians removed from the East Coast.
Sentence 5: Unlike other wartime immigration detention stations, Ellis Island was designated as a permanent holding facility and was used to hold foreign nationals throughout the war.
