Sentence 1: Innocence Project
Innocence Project The Innocence Project is a non-profit legal organization that is committed to exonerating wrongly convicted people through the use of DNA testing and to reforming the criminal justice system to prevent future injustice.
Sentence 2: The group cites various studies estimating that in the United States, between 2.3 and 5% of all prisoners are innocent.
Sentence 3: The Innocence Project was founded in 1992 by Barry Scheck and Peter Neufeld.
Sentence 4: The work of the Innocence Project has led to the freeing of more than 350 wrongfully convicted people based on DNA, including 20 individuals who spent time on death row, and the
Sentence 5: Georgia Innocence Project
Georgia Innocence Project The Georgia Innocence Project is a non-profit corporation based in Decatur, Georgia, United States.
Sentence 6: Its mission "is to free the wrongly prosecuted through DNA testing, to advance practices that minimize the chances that others suffer the same fate, to educate the public that wrongful convictions are not rare or isolated events, and to help the exonerated rebuild their lives."
Sentence 7: Georgia Innocence Project is now headed by Executive Director Clare Gilbert.
