Sentence 1: Colossus computer
Colossus computer Colossus was a set of computers developed by British codebreakers in the years 1943–1945 to help in the cryptanalysis of the Lorenz cipher.
Sentence 2: Colossus used thermionic valves (vacuum tubes) to perform Boolean and counting operations.
Sentence 3: Colossus is thus regarded as the world's first programmable, electronic, digital computer, although it was programmed by switches and plugs and not by a stored program.
Sentence 4: Colossus was designed by research telephone engineer Tommy Flowers to solve a problem posed by mathematician Max Newman at the Government Code and Cypher School (GC&CS) at Bletchley Park.
Sentence 5: Computer
Colossus.
Sentence 6: After a functional test in December 1943, Colossus was shipped to Bletchley Park, where it was delivered on 18 January 1944 and attacked its first message on 5 February.
Sentence 7: Colossus was the world's first electronic digital programmable computer.
